3	/**
4	 * What a policy covers: the items the office recorded when it wrote the policy,
5	 * able to say which of them a reported damage is settled on.
6	 *
7	 * Reading a damage report against the covered items is a decision of its own,
8	 * revised on its own schedule and quite apart from what one damage earns or what
9	 * the cap allows. Two pending revisions are this coverage's alone: counting a
10	 * policy's several items of one kind against the damages reported for that kind,
11	 * and refusing a damage that names a kind the policy does not cover. Neither
12	 * touches the claim rules or the cap.
13	 */
14	record Coverage(List<InsuredItem> insuredItems) {
15	
16	    static Coverage of(List<InsuredItem> insuredItems) {
17	        return new Coverage(List.copyOf(insuredItems));
18	    }
19	
20	    /**
21	     * The covered item whose terms a reported damage is settled on: the office
22	     * looks the reported kind of item up among the items it covers, and the
23	     * claim rules read the clauses from it. A damage naming a kind of item the
24	     * coverage does not include is no claim the office will settle.
25	     *
26	     * The office matches by the kind of item reported, which is all a single
27	     * covered item of a kind needs. Counting several items of one kind against
28	     * the damages reported for that kind is a separate decision the office has
29	     * not been asked to make yet.
30	     */
31	    InsuredItem itemDamagedBy(Damage damage) {
32	        for (InsuredItem insuredItem : insuredItems) {
33	            if (insuredItem.type().equals(damage.itemType())) {
34	                return insuredItem;
35	            }
36	        }
37	        throw new IllegalArgumentException(
38	                "the policy does not cover a " + damage.itemType());
39	    }
40	}
